<FinancingDossier
  title="AI infrastructure financing"
  description="A reading path through AI investment: customer commitments, data-centre debt, equipment collateral and the accounting estimates behind the financing."
  atlas={{ image: '/illustrations/news/openai-credit-guarantee-v1.jpg', href: '/en/atlas/ai-financing/', title: 'AI financing atlas', note: 'Explore dated relationships around Nvidia, OpenAI, SB Energy and AIP. Each relationship retains its source, contractual status and evidential limits; this is a selected network, rather than an inventory of the entire industry.' }}
  analyses={analyses}
  guides={guides}
  related={{ href: '/en/topics/private-credit-insurance/', title: 'Private credit and insurance' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="mechanism">
    <p>
      An AI service reaches its customer through a physical and financial chain: chips and buildings require investment, electricity sustains operations, and customer payments support the companies’ obligations. The financing may sit with a cloud operator, a landlord or a dedicated project vehicle. Start by identifying the borrower and its sources of repayment, then follow the contract linking each party to the next.
    </p>
    <p>
      A loan secured against infrastructure can also depend on a customer contract. In its <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1769628/000176962826000129/ex991.htm">March 2026 financing announcement filed with the SEC</a>, CoreWeave describes security over infrastructure and an associated customer contract, held within a dedicated borrower. That issuer statement documents the announced arrangement; the scope and enforceability of individual rights require the underlying agreements. A pledged machine, access to its building and the cash generated by its use belong to separate parts of the analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      The investment timetable also differs from the income statement. Equipment purchases use cash, while depreciation spreads their recognised cost over an estimated useful life. Amazon’s <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/000101872426000004/amzn-20251231.htm">2025 annual filing</a> illustrates how a revised estimate changes the expense recorded for part of a fleet. Accounting useful life, technical serviceability and resale value answer different questions. Read them alongside repayment dates and customer obligations to assess whether the financing assumptions fit together.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="limits">
    <p>
      This dossier connects cases with different legal entities, assets and reporting periods. It provides a method for reading the documents, without treating the companies as a single consolidated borrower. The investigations retain their own research dates; a historical announcement describes its documented stage and carries no automatic assurance of later completion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public filings disclose only part of the contractual picture. Undisclosed termination rights, restrictions on asset transfers and recovery costs can remain material unknowns. The <a href="https://pcaobus.org/oversight/standards/auditing-standards/details/AS2501">PCAOB standard on auditing accounting estimates</a> addresses methods, assumptions and potential bias; it supplies an audit framework rather than a universal service life for AI equipment. A stress scenario therefore needs explicit assumptions about utilisation, customer payments, refinancing and recoveries before it can support a loss estimate.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FinancingDossier>
